1. Conclusion
The announced invention points in our invention are fundamentally different from Cite 1, Cite 2, and Cite 3. The invention cannot be derived from Cite 1, Cite 2, Cite 3, or any combination thereof.
In particular, none of the cited references discloses or suggests: (a) generating a quiet-oriented future motion and correcting its real execution through a phase-aware quiet tracking framework; (b) estimating a quiet CoM (Center of Mass) support-transfer state from the future motion together with the actual execution history, and generating a quiet correction token and a current support-transition phase; (c) phase-conditioned joint-responsibility decoding in which different joints are assigned correction responsibility according to the support-transfer phase; or (d) phase-aware routing of fast (foot-ground contact) and slow (robot-side) dynamics for quiet-action decoding. The combination of Cite 1 + Cite 2 (pointed out in the search report) only covers conventional balance/ZMP feedback control and a rule-based torso posture lookup table during a single-leg support (grasping) period; it neither targets low-noise (quiet) touchdown execution nor discloses any learned, phase-aware support-transfer correction architecture.

2. The summary of the invention
Our invention is designed to make a humanoid robot actually walk quietly (low footstep impact / low vibration) in shared living spaces. The key technical problem is as follows. A quiet-oriented future motion can be planned in advance (e.g., lower touchdown velocity, smoother swing-leg deceleration, a gentler support transition). However, when this planned quiet motion is executed on the real robot, the actual touchdown is often NOT as quiet as planned: changes in payload, actuator response/delay, robot state, or foot-ground contact condition (ground stiffness, friction, contact timing, slip) cause the new support foot to accept the body load too quickly, producing a large impact peak and a loud footstep. In short, the two major issues are: 1) a planned quiet future motion is not always executed quietly on the real robot; and 2) the controller must keep the touchdown quiet even though the robot-side (slow) and contact-side (fast) physical conditions change at runtime.
[Fig. placeholder] Fig. 1  The problem: the same planned quiet future motion results in a soft touchdown (left) versus a heavy/loud touchdown (right) when the execution condition changes.
The present invention relates to a method for a humanoid robot to perform stable and quiet landing based on a phase-aware, low-noise (vibration-free) tracking framework, comprising:
(1) generating a future motion aimed at quiet operation from a task instruction, a target motion, or a motion reference, and generating the robot's current (base) motion based on the future motion;
(2) estimating a stable Center of Mass (CoM) state required for the current phase based on the future motion and the actual execution history, and generating a low-noise (vibration-free) adjustment (correction) token and the current support-transition phase; and
(3) generating joint correction values conditioned on the phase and dynamics, and combining the low-noise (vibration-free) correction with the base motion to obtain the final robot motion.
[Fig. placeholder] Fig. 2  The solution of our invention: a dedicated quiet-correction branch added on top of a future-motion-conditioned tracking policy.
The main innovations of our invention:
1. Phase-Aware Quiet CoM Prior Correction - a recurrent (GRU) support-transfer reasoner fuses sliding-window future motion with the actual execution history through future-execution cross-attention, estimates the quiet CoM support-transfer state required at the current step, compares it with the base CoM trend via a difference learner and a context-aware gate, and outputs a compact quiet CoM-correction token together with the current support-transition phase (Phase A: unload & CoM preparation, Phase B: light-contact isolation, Phase C: smooth load acceptance). (Fig. 3)
2. Phase-Gated Dynamic Joint-Responsibility Decoding - a phase-conditioned joint-responsibility router converts the correction token and phase weights into per-joint responsibility maps, and phase-specialized quiet residual experts (for CoM preparation, contact isolation, and load acceptance) decode phase-weighted residual actions that are softly combined by the phase weights. (Fig. 4)
3. Phase-Aware Fast-Slow Dynamics Routing - a slow dynamics encoder estimates slowly varying robot-side conditions (payload, actuator response/delay, stiffness, damping) and a fast dynamics encoder estimates short-term foot-ground responses (touchdown timing, contact hardness, friction, slip); a phase-aware router assigns phase-dependent relevance weights to the fast/slow dynamics features and fuses them into a dynamics context (e.g., via FiLM modulation) for the quiet residual controller. (Fig. 5)
Note (baseline technology disclaimer): future-motion-conditioned tracking frameworks such as SONIC are publicly known execution backbones. The innovation of our invention does NOT lie in the use of such a future-motion tracking framework itself, but rather in the three dedicated quiet-correction mechanisms described above, which make the planned quiet motion actually be executed quietly under changing physical conditions. These distinguishing features are not disclosed in any of Cite 1, Cite 2, or Cite 3.

3. Comparison Analysis
Cite 1: CN 122755662 A
The summary of Cite 1
Cite 1 discloses a center-of-gravity control method for a humanoid robot during a single-leg support period, in the context of a GRASPING operation (switching from a one-hand grasp to a two-hand grasp on an object). Its working flow is: acquire an initial surface image of the target object and extract the surface texture roughness to obtain a friction type; read a preset action-scheme library according to the friction type to obtain a torso forward-tilt intervention timing, a contact confirmation condition, and a reverse-tension trigger condition; collect the contact pressure signal at the manipulator end and compute the pressure rise rate to evaluate the adhesion, generating the grasp-switch timing; before the switch timing arrives, perform a torso forward-tilt trial action while collecting the torque outputs of the hip, knee, and ankle joints of the support leg and denoising them to obtain a stable torque waveform, analyzing how much the torso forward tilt is obstructed; and finally combine the current torso inclination angle, the time phase of the support-leg ankle torque (the phase position at which the ankle torque peak appears), and the obstruction degree, read an adjustment margin from a preset posture-margin lookup table, and generate a target torso forward-tilt action. In simple terms, Cite 1 prevents the robot from tipping forward while it leans forward to grasp an object during single-leg support, by looking up a preset safety margin table.
[Fig. placeholder] Fig. 6  The working flow of Cite 1 (grasping-oriented center-of-gravity control).
Difference:
The differences with Cite 1 are listed as follows:
1) The purposes are different.
Cite 1 aims at maintaining balance (preventing forward tipping) during a single-leg support GRASPING action, by correcting the torso posture within a preset safety margin. The present invention aims at realizing a planned quiet (low-noise, low-impact) support transfer during WALKING on the real robot: it ensures that a quiet-oriented future motion is still executed quietly when the payload, actuator response, or foot-ground condition changes, so as to reduce footstep impact noise and vibration in shared living spaces. The application scenario (grasping balance versus quiet walking/landing) and the control objective (anti-tipping versus low-impact touchdown) are both different.
2) Technical solutions are different.
(a) Cite 1 uses a rule-based preset posture-margin lookup table driven by the current torso inclination and the ankle-torque peak phase of the support leg; it has no future motion, no execution-history reasoning, and no learned estimation network. The invention uses a recurrent (GRU) reasoner with future-execution cross-attention to estimate the quiet CoM support-transfer state from the future motion plus the actual execution history, and generates a learned correction token through a difference learner, a context-aware gate, and a bottleneck projection.
(b) Cite 1 generates only a target torso forward-tilt action for the support leg; it does not estimate a stable CoM state, does not generate any adjustment/correction token, does not assign phase-conditioned joint responsibility across the whole body, and does not model or route fast/slow dynamics. The invention generates whole-body joint correction values conditioned on the phase and on fast (contact-side) and slow (robot-side) dynamics contexts, and adds the resulting quiet residual to the base motion (a_t = a_base + delta_a_quiet).
3) The "phase" of Cite 1 and the "phase" of our invention are different concepts.
The "ankle torque time phase" of Cite 1 is merely the phase position of the ankle-torque peak within the support period, used only as an index to read the lookup table; it does not partition the support transfer into functional stages and does not generate any phase weight. The "support-transition phase" of the invention is a predicted, continuous, three-stage weighting vector g_t = [g_A, g_B, g_C] (unload & CoM preparation / light-contact isolation / smooth load acceptance), which is itself estimated by the recurrent reasoner and is used both to gate joint responsibility and to route dynamics features. In simple terms, Cite 1's "phase" is a single time instant for reading a table, whereas our "phase" is a learned, stage-wise control signal that changes which joints act and which dynamics are trusted at each moment of the touchdown.
[Fig. placeholder] Fig. 7  Concept comparison: Cite 1 ankle-torque peak phase point (a single index) versus our continuous three-stage phase weight g_t.
4) Effects are different.
Cite 1 improves dynamic balance (anti-tipping) during a single-leg support grasping action. The invention reduces touchdown impact and footstep noise and improves the robustness of quiet execution under varying payloads, robot states, and ground conditions, while still preserving balance.

Cite 2: KR 10-0927572 B1
The summary of Cite 2
Cite 2 discloses a balance control system and method for a biped walking robot. Its working flow is: a walking motion trajectory is generated based on an inverted pendulum model; a Kalman filter predicts the ZMP value of the next command cycle; the predicted ZMP value is compared with the actual ZMP value measured by pressure sensors to compute an error; the error is converted into a COM value, and a control signal is output to compensate the angular velocity of the joints, thereby keeping the ZMP within the stable region of the support polygon. In simple terms, Cite 2 nudges the joint angular velocities only after the measured ZMP has already deviated from the target, i.e., it is a reactive (after-the-fact) balance feedback.
[Fig. placeholder] Fig. 8  The working flow of Cite 2 (ZMP-error feedback balance control).
Difference:
The differences with Cite 2 are listed as follows:
1) The purposes are different.
Cite 2 aims at balance/disturbance-rejection control, i.e., keeping the ZMP inside the stable support region so that the robot does not fall. The present invention aims at quiet execution: reproducing the planned quiet support-transfer process on the real robot to reduce foot-ground impact, noise, and vibration during touchdown and load acceptance.
2) Technical solutions are different.
(a) Cite 2 is a model-based feedback scheme: inverted pendulum model + Kalman-filter ZMP prediction + ZMP error-to-COM conversion + joint angular-velocity compensation. It does not consider any task instruction, target motion, or motion reference; it generates no future-motion-conditioned base motion and no adjustment token; and it does not consider any phase. The invention is a learning-based, future-motion-conditioned framework in which a quiet correction token and the current support-transition phase are generated by a recurrent future-execution reasoning module, and phase/dynamics-conditioned joint corrections are combined with the base motion.
(b) Cite 2 reacts to the ZMP error of the current/next command cycle only; it has no notion of preparing the support transfer in advance (CoM preparation before touchdown), no stage-wise division of the touchdown process, no per-phase joint-responsibility assignment, and no fast/slow dynamics adaptation. The invention explicitly prepares the quiet support transfer in advance (Phase A), isolates the light contact (Phase B), and smoothly accepts the load (Phase C), with joint responsibility and dynamics routing changing phase by phase.
3) The "correction" of Cite 2 and the "correction" of our invention are different in nature (reactive versus proactive).
The correction of Cite 2 is a reactive feedback: it compensates the joint angular velocities only in response to a ZMP error that has already occurred (its stated motivation is to overcome the time delay of ZMP measurement and compensation). The correction of the invention is proactive: the CoM and the support-transfer state are prepared in advance before the foot lands (Phase A), so that the touchdown is quiet by design rather than corrected after an impact has happened. In simple terms, Cite 2 corrects a deviation after it appears, whereas our invention prepares the support transfer in advance so that the impact does not occur in the first place.
4) The "CoM" of Cite 2 and the "CoM state" of our invention are different in meaning.
In Cite 2, the COM value is a scalar compensation quantity obtained by converting the ZMP error, used only to compute the joint angular-velocity compensation. In the invention, the CoM state is the quiet support-transfer state that is required for the current phase and is estimated from the future motion together with the execution history; it is used to derive a quiet correction token, not merely a scalar error feedback.
5) Effects are different.
Cite 2 improves resistance to external disturbances (anti-fall). The invention reduces the peak contact force and footstep noise at touchdown and keeps the quiet behavior consistent under changing payload, actuator, and ground conditions; balance is a prerequisite, not the objective.

Cite 3: KR 10-2026-0082937 A
The summary of Cite 3
Cite 3 discloses a robot control method based on a simplified map and a natural-language command. Its working flow is: acquire an image of a space, a simplified map (in which some elements are omitted) representing the robot's current and destination locations, and a natural-language command describing requirements the robot must follow during operation; generate an input token from at least one of the image, the simplified map, and the command; and process the input token with a learned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) model to generate waypoint tokens, so that the robot can respond to unexpected situations. In simple terms, Cite 3 plans where the robot should go (navigation waypoints), not how each footstep physically lands.
[Fig. placeholder] Fig. 9  The working flow of Cite 3 (VLA-based navigation waypoint generation).
Difference:
The differences with Cite 3 are listed as follows:
1) The purposes are different.
Cite 3 is a high-level navigation/task-planning technique: it converts perception (image/map) and natural-language task constraints into waypoints so that the robot reaches a destination while complying with the command. The present invention is a low-level motion-execution (tracking) technique: given an already-generated quiet-oriented future motion, it corrects the real execution so that the physical touchdown remains quiet.
2) Technical solutions are different.
(a) Cite 3 operates at the waypoint/planning level: its model input is an image, a simplified map, and a natural-language command. It contains no future-motion tracking policy, no CoM state estimation, no support-transition phase, no joint-level correction, and no dynamics modeling. The invention operates at the whole-body joint-control level: the correction token is generated from the future motion and the proprioceptive execution history, and phase-conditioned joint residuals are added to the base joint action.
(b) In Cite 3 the natural-language command only corresponds to the "task instruction" of the invention (an input that may trigger motion generation); Cite 3 says nothing about how the motion is physically executed, let alone how a quiet support transfer is estimated or corrected.
3) The "token" of Cite 3 and the "token" of our invention are different in level and function.
The tokens of Cite 3 are navigation waypoint tokens: they describe positions the robot should move toward, produced by a VLA model from an image/map/command. The token of the invention is a quiet CoM-correction token: it encodes the deviation between the base CoM trend and the required quiet CoM support-transfer state, and is used to drive phase-weighted joint residual corrections. In simple terms, Cite 3's token decides "where to go", whereas our token decides "how to land this footstep quietly".
4) Effects are different.
Cite 3 improves the efficiency and flexibility of robot control tasks (navigation under natural-language constraints). The invention reduces touchdown impact/noise and improves quiet-execution robustness of humanoid walking under changing physical conditions.

Note on the combination of Cite 1 + Cite 2 (and Cite 3)
The search report considers the inventiveness of the present invention to be significantly low over the combination of Cite 1 and Cite 2. However:
(a) Even combined, Cite 1 (rule-based torso lookup-table correction within a single-leg support grasping action) and Cite 2 (reactive ZMP-error feedback balance control) only provide conventional reactive balance control. Neither of them discloses generating a quiet-oriented future motion and a base motion from it, estimating a quiet CoM support-transfer state from the future motion + execution history, generating a quiet correction token, or predicting a support-transition phase (g_t).
(b) There is no motivation to combine them into the claimed phase-aware quiet tracking framework: a person skilled in the art starting from Cite 1's ankle-torque peak phase and Cite 2's reactive ZMP feedback would arrive at a balance controller, not at a learned, phase-gated, dynamics-routed quiet correction branch that prepares the support transfer in advance and softly combines phase-specialized residual experts by continuous phase weights. In particular, the invention's "phase" is a predicted, continuous, three-stage (unload & CoM preparation / light-contact isolation / smooth load acceptance) weighting that gates both joint-responsibility decoding and fast/slow dynamics routing, which is fundamentally different from Cite 1's ankle-torque peak phase and is absent from Cite 2.
(c) Cite 3 only additionally provides a VLA-based navigation waypoint generator; it does not bridge the gap toward phase-aware quiet execution either. Therefore, the invention cannot be derived from the combination of Cite 1, Cite 2, and Cite 3.

As a result, the invention cannot be derived from the combination of the one of Cite 1, Cite 2, Cite 3.
